{"name":"Melt With You","hex":"#e3cfab","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"melt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To change (or to be changed) from a solid state to a liquid state, usually by a gradual heat.","To dissolve, disperse, vanish."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Molten material, the product of melting.","The transition of matter from a solid state to a liquid state."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melt"},{"term":"with","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Preposition","definitions":["Against.","In the company of; alongside, close to; near to."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Along, together with others, in a group, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/with"},{"term":"you","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Pronoun","definitions":["The people spoken, or written to, as an object. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","(To) yourselves, (to) yourself."]},{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["The individual or group spoken or written to.","Used before epithets, describing the person being addressed, for emphasis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/you"}],"entries":[{"title":"I Melt with You","blocks":[{"text":"\"I Melt with You\" is a song by the British new wave band Modern English. The song, produced by Hugh Jones, was the second single from their 1982 album After the Snow. \nIt became the band's most successful single, largely in the United States, where it was featured in the film Valley Girl and on MTV. It reached number seven on Billboard's Mainstream Rock chart in 1983 and a re-release reached number 76 on its Hot 100 chart in 1990."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/89/I_Melt_with_You_by_Modern_English_1982_original_release.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Melt_with_You","wikidata":"Q5978440"},{"title":"You","blocks":[{"text":"In Modern English, the word \"you\" is the second-person pronoun. It is grammatically plural, and was historically used only for the dative case, but in most modern dialects is used for all cases and numbers."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You","wikidata":"Q1165897"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"melt":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/En-us-melt.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/eb/En-us-melt.ogg/En-us-melt.ogg.mp3"}],"with":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/En-uk-with.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/0/00/En-uk-with.ogg/En-uk-with.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/En-us-with.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/42/En-us-with.ogg/En-us-with.ogg.mp3"}],"you":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/En-us-you_unstressed.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/80/En-us-you_unstressed.ogg/En-us-you_unstressed.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/En-uk-you.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/13/En-uk-you.ogg/En-uk-you.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-you.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionar